{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>My statutes you shall keep.</b> And these are: ״You shall not breed your animal kil'aim,״ and so on. חֻקִּים — these are decrees of the King that have no reason." "<b>And a kil'aim garment.</b> Why is this stated? Since it is stated ״You shall not wear sha'atnez, wool and linen together״ (Deuteronomy 22), one might think he may not wear fleeces of wool and flax fibers — Scripture states ״בֶּגֶד״. From where to include felt? Scripture states ״שַׁעַטְנֵז״ — a thing that is שׁוּעַ spun and נוּז. And I say: נוּז is a term for something combed and woven, this with that, to join it — טִישְׁטִ\"יר בְּלַעַ\"ז — as in ״חַזְיָין לִנְזָאֵי דְאִית בְּהוֹן״ (Mo'ed Katan 12), which we explain as a term for carding, פְלִישְׁטְרָא, and the term שַׁעַטְנֵז, Menachem explained: joining wool and linen."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "Rashi cites Mo'ed Katan 12a; the Aramaic phrase there (לנזי דאית בהון) refers to garments that have kil'aim in them. פְלִישְׁטְרָא is likely Old French. The la'az טִישְׁטִ\"יר may correspond to Old French 'tistre' ('to weave'). פְלִישְׁטְרָא may correspond to Old French 'pelettier' or a carding-related term."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:15.057131904Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}